const PASSAGE_F = `The concept of authority has long occupied a central place in political and moral philosophy, yet its foundations remain deeply contested. Authority, in the standard philosophical sense, is not merely power — the capacity to compel compliance through force or incentive. Authority is normative: it gives those subject to it a reason to comply that is independent of whether they would have chosen to comply on the merits of the particular directive. If a legitimate authority tells me to do something I would not otherwise have done, I have a reason — the directive itself — to do it. This is what philosophers call a "content-independent" reason: the reason to obey derives from the source of the directive, not from the content of what is directed.

The classical liberal tradition, extending from Locke through Kant, located the foundation of authority in consent and rational autonomy. Authority is legitimate when it derives from the rational will of those subject to it — either because they have actually consented or because they would consent under idealized conditions of full information and rational deliberation. On this view, there is a deep connection between political authority and personal autonomy: legitimate authority does not simply override the will of the subject but in some sense expresses it.

Joseph Raz challenged this account with what he called the "service conception" of authority. On Raz's view, an authority is legitimate when its directives help those subject to it to conform better to reasons they already have — reasons that apply to them independently of the authority. A state that directs traffic does not override drivers' autonomy; it helps each driver achieve a goal — arriving safely — that the driver already has. The state's authority, on the service conception, is justified not by consent but by the fact that following the authority's directives produces better outcomes (relative to independently existing reasons) than each person acting on their own judgment.

Critics of Raz's account have pressed two objections. The first is epistemic: who determines whether an authority's directives actually produce better conformity with independent reasons? The question cannot be answered by the authority itself — that would be circular — and it is not obvious that any neutral third party can reliably assess it. If subjects must themselves evaluate whether the authority's track record justifies deference, then the authority's claim to replace their individual judgment collapses back into a requirement that subjects exercise exactly the independent judgment that deference was supposed to suspend.

The second objection is political: the service conception seems to permit — or even require — highly paternalistic forms of authority, wherever the authority genuinely produces better outcomes than individual judgment. If a benevolent and well-informed autocracy consistently made better decisions for its subjects than they would make for themselves, the service conception would appear to legitimate it. Yet most liberal political philosophers regard such legitimation as deeply problematic — not merely because benevolent autocracies are empirically rare, but because there seems to be a value in political self-determination that is not reducible to getting better outcomes.`

const PASSAGE_G = `In the philosophy of history, there is a persistent debate about what it means to understand a past event — to understand it, that is, in the way that a historian rather than a mere chronicler seeks to understand it. The chronicler records: this happened, then this happened, then this happened. The historian, by contrast, seeks to explain: why did this happen, what conditions made it possible, what did it mean to those who participated in it, and how did it produce what came after? But the gap between chronicle and explanation conceals a series of contested methodological commitments about how historical understanding is possible.

One influential tradition holds that historical understanding requires what Wilhelm Dilthey called "verstehen" — a kind of empathetic re-enactment or imaginative reconstruction of the mental states of historical actors. To understand why Caesar crossed the Rubicon, we must imaginatively inhabit the practical situation Caesar faced, grasp the considerations as they appeared to him, and recover the deliberative process by which he arrived at his decision. History, on this view, is fundamentally the study of human minds under conditions different from our own, and the historian's distinctive tool is empathetic imagination rather than causal analysis.

The verstehen tradition faced a powerful methodological objection: how can we be confident that our imaginative reconstruction of a historical actor's mental states is accurate rather than a projection of our own assumptions? The further a historical period is from our own — in time, culture, economic organization, and basic conceptual frameworks — the less reliable our empathetic imagination is likely to be. Medieval peasants may have organized their experience through conceptual categories so different from our own that imaginative re-enactment produces not accurate understanding but systematic distortion dressed in the costume of insight.

A rival tradition, associated with the social sciences, proposed that historical explanation should be modeled on causal explanation in natural science. On this view, the historian's task is to identify the general laws or structural factors — economic, demographic, ecological — that causally produce the patterns of history. Individual actors and their mental states are epiphenomenal: what matters is not what Caesar thought but what structural conditions made the particular event he participated in virtually inevitable. Understanding history means identifying causes, not re-enacting deliberations.

Critics of the scientific model pointed out that historical events, unlike natural events, involve the actions of agents who responded to their situations under some description. Even if demographic pressure caused a migration pattern, the migration was enacted by people who understood themselves as fleeing drought, seeking opportunity, or fulfilling religious obligation — and their self-understanding was causally relevant to how the migration occurred and what it produced. Eliminating the intentional dimension of historical events does not produce more rigorous understanding; it eliminates something that is constitutively part of what there is to understand.`

const PASSAGE_H = `The question of what obligations, if any, the present generation owes to future generations is among the most philosophically difficult in contemporary ethics. The difficulty is not merely practical — though the practical difficulties of predicting what future generations will need are severe enough — but conceptual. Most of our ethical frameworks presuppose reciprocity: we owe obligations to those who can also bear obligations toward us, with whom we are engaged in a cooperative scheme of mutual benefit. Future people cannot bear obligations to the present generation because they do not yet exist; they cannot participate in the cooperative schemes from which our ordinary obligations arise; and they cannot consent to the arrangements we make that will affect them.

Derek Parfit's "non-identity problem" adds a further layer of difficulty. Any specific energy policy we might adopt today — policies that will affect future emissions, resource availability, and climate conditions — will influence which people come to exist in the future. A world in which we adopt aggressive emissions controls will be populated by a different set of people (conceived in different circumstances, by different parents who met in different ways) than a world in which we do not. If future people exist only because of the policies we adopt, it is difficult to say that those policies harmed them — since, had we adopted different policies, these particular people would not have existed at all, and cannot therefore have been wronged by having been brought into existence.

This result seems paradoxical: even if a policy leads to environmental degradation that leaves future people significantly worse off than they might otherwise be, those people cannot coherently complain that they were wronged, since the alternative would have been their non-existence. Parfit called this a "repugnant conclusion" — though his own repugnant conclusion was different, concerning population ethics — because it seems to permit almost any present policy as long as it produces a future population that considers life worth living.

Several responses have been proposed. Some philosophers invoke a rights framework: even future persons who exist because of our choices have rights against the conditions we bequeath them, and violating those rights cannot be justified by noting that they would not have existed otherwise. Others appeal to impersonal consequentialism: the relevant question is not whether identifiable future individuals are harmed, but whether the world we create is better or worse by some impersonal standard. Still others abandon the attempt to ground future obligations in harm to identifiable individuals and instead ground them in present responsibilities — what kind of people do we want to be, and what do our commitments to sustainability say about our character and values, regardless of who will benefit?

Each of these responses faces its own difficulties. The rights framework must explain why rights-bearers need not be identifiable or currently existing. Impersonal consequentialism must explain how to compare worlds containing entirely different people. And the virtue or character-based approach must explain why virtues that produce no benefit to any person — present or future — constitute genuine moral reasons for action. The non-identity problem remains one of the most productive, and most genuinely unresolved, problems in contemporary ethics.`

const PASSAGE_I = `The relationship between scientific expertise and democratic governance poses one of the most persistent dilemmas of liberal political theory. Democracy rests on the premise that political authority derives from the consent and participation of the governed; yet much of what modern governments must decide — how to regulate nuclear waste, which vaccines to mandate, what climate targets to set — depends on technical knowledge that most citizens do not possess and cannot, in the time available, acquire. The result is a structural tension: democratic legitimacy seems to require popular participation, but effective governance in technical domains seems to require deference to experts.

Harry Collins and Robert Evans proposed that this tension can be addressed by distinguishing different kinds of expertise. "Interactional expertise" is the ability to talk with specialists in a field without being able to practice it — the science journalist who understands enough physics to ask useful questions but cannot run the experiments. "Contributory expertise" is the ability to actually practice a field — the physicist who runs the experiments. On Collins and Evans's account, only contributory experts should have decision-making authority in technical domains within their field. Interactional experts — including most policy makers and members of the public — should participate in the framing of questions but defer to contributory experts on technical questions of fact.

Critics have raised two objections to this framework. First, the distinction between technical questions of fact and policy questions of value is difficult to maintain in practice. The question of whether a particular pesticide is "safe" is superficially a technical question but actually involves value-laden choices about which risks count, at what exposure level, for which populations. Experts can establish the dose-response relationship; they cannot, without making value choices, determine what level of risk to a particular population is acceptable. The framing of the technical question already embeds normative assumptions that experts are no more qualified than laypeople to make.

Second, the history of expert consensus has not been unblemished. Medical authorities recommended thalidomide; economists endorsed austerity policies that proved catastrophic; nutrition scientists promoted low-fat dietary guidelines that have since been revised. These are not merely cases of incorrect expertise — they illustrate how expert communities can develop systematic biases from their institutional positions, funding sources, and shared disciplinary assumptions. If expert consensus is itself subject to systematic distortion, then a democratic check on expert authority serves not merely a legitimacy function but a corrective epistemic one.

The most defensible position may lie between technocratic deference and populist rejection of expertise. Neither dismissing expert consensus as merely one opinion among many nor treating it as infallible resolves the underlying tension. What is needed is institutional design that incorporates genuine expert input — including expert disagreement, which is often suppressed in public-facing communications — while preserving meaningful opportunities for democratic deliberation about the value choices that are inevitably embedded in technical decisions.`

